1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,530
Your AI agents fail in production.
How do you make them reliably better — and prove it?

2
00:00:05,880 --> 00:00:18,790
Meet cap-evolve. It runs a continuous reflective loop:
evaluating traces, clustering failures, proposing bold edits,
gating every change on held-out splits, and committing the wins to git.

3
00:00:19,140 --> 00:00:32,330
It runs from a single command: freeze the splits,
measure an honest baseline, then propose one candidate at a time.
Every candidate faces the same gate: accept, reject, or not enough evidence.

4
00:00:32,680 --> 00:00:42,110
Every run is fully inspectable. Nine tabs — candidates, gate,
tasks, diffs and cost — so you can see exactly how the capability evolved.

5
00:00:42,450 --> 00:00:55,360
On τ²-bench airline, reward climbed from fifty-three point six
to seventy-one point two percent — a thirty-two point eight percent
relative gain, with five of ten iterations accepted by the gate.

6
00:00:55,710 --> 00:01:08,670
On the Red Hat Software Engineering benchmark, an optimized
Sonnet 4.6 rose from fifty-five point seven to seventy-three point one.
Thirty-one percent better, and past an unoptimized Opus.

7
00:01:09,020 --> 00:01:18,220
Agents optimizing agents, under strict cost constraints.
Every iteration lives in git-committed markdown you can read, diff, and revert.

8
00:01:18,570 --> 00:01:22,700
Optimize your own agent today.
Get started with cap-evolve on GitHub.
